        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "This project contains qualitative data from seven focus groups with 29 Hispanic and Latino workers in Wichita and Garden City, Kansas. These communities experience frequent extreme weather and have large bilingual populations. The focus groups explore how multilingual workers understand and respond to workplace risks during extreme weather events.\nThe discussions document workers’ experiences with limited access to hazard information in their dominant language, unclear or inconsistent workplace emergency preparedness practices, and workplace power dynamics that can discourage protective action. Participants also shared ideas for improving workplace safety during hazardous weather, including stronger preparedness planning, more consistent multilingual communication, and better infrastructure.\nThis dataset can support research on extreme weather risk communication, occupational safety, social vulnerability, and disaster preparedness. It may also be useful for policymakers, emergency managers, and community organizations working to improve workplace safety and hazard communication for multilingual communities.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          },
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "The Demographic Information Sheet was used to collect anonymous background information from participants involved in the study. The questionnaire gathered basic demographic details such as age, gender, country of origin, and length of residence in Kansas. It also included questions about confidence in English in speaking, understanding, and reading, as well as the languages participants use at work and at home. Additional items asked about participants’ industry of employment and the length of time in their current position. This information helped provide context for the focus group discussions and supports the analysis of how language, work environments, and migration experiences relate to extreme weather preparedness and risk communication. All information were de-identified for the purpose of this project, so while the team cannot provide the direct responses, we hope the questionnaire is useful for future use.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
        ],
